//# Notations on the hardcopy document read, in part:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:25
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:26
//#    Assemble revision 001 of AGC program LMY99 by NASA 2021112-061
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:27
//#    16:27 JULY 14, 1969
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:28
//
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:29
//## <b>Note:</b> Notations below resembling this note are 21st-century
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:30
//## annotations added by the <a href="http://www.ibiblio.org/apollo">
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:31
//## Virtual AGC project</a>, and are not original AGC source code.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:32
//# Page 390
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:33
//# PROGRAM NAME -- KEYBOARD AND DISPLAY PROGRAM
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:34
//# MOD NO -- 4		DATE -- 27 APRIL 1967		ASSEMBLY -- PINDANCE REV 18
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:35
//# MOD BY -- FILENE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:36
//# LOG SECTION -- PINBALL GAME BUTTONS AND LIGHTS
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:37
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:38
//# FUNCTIONAL DESCRIPTION
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:39
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:40
//# THE KEYBOARD AND DISPLAY SYSTEM PROGRAM OPERATES UNDER EXECUTIVE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:41
//# CONTROL AND PROCESSES INFORMATION EXCHANGED BETWEEN THE AGC AND THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:42
//# COMPUTER OPERATOR.  THE INPUTS TO THE PROGRAM ARE FROM THE KEYBOARD,
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:43
//# FROM INTERNAL PROGRAM, AND FROM THE UPLINK.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:44
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:45
//# THE LANGUAGE OF COMMUNICATION WITH THE PROGRAM IS A PAIR OF WORDS
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:46
//# KNOWN AS VERB AND NOUN.  EACH OF THESE IS REPRESENTED BY A 2 CHARACTER
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:47
//# DECIMAL NUMBER.  THE VERB CODE INDICATES WHAT ACTION IS TO BE TAKEN, THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:48
//# NOUN CODE INDICATES TO WHAT THIS ACTION IS APPLIED.  NOUNS USUALLY
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:49
//# REFER TO A GROUP OF ERASABLE REGISTERS.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:50
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:51
//# VERBS ARE GROUPED INTO DISPLAYS, LOADS, MONITORS (DISPLAYS THAT ARE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:52
//# UPDATED ONCE PER SECOND), SPECIAL FUNCTIONS, AND EXTENDED VERBS (THESE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:53
//# ARE OUTSIDE OF THE DOMAIN OF PINBALL AND CAN BE FOUND UNDER LOG SECTION
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:54
//# 'EXTENDED VERBS').
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:55
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:56
//# A LIST OF VERBS AND NOUNS IS GIVEN IN LOG SECTION 'ASSEMBLY AND
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:57
//# OPERATION INFORMATION'.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:58
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:59
//## Ram&oacute;n Alonso, one of the original AGC developers, provides a
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:60
//## little more insight:  Apparently, nobody had yet arrived at any kind
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:61
//## of software requirements for the AGC's user interface when the desire
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:62
//## arose within the Instrumentation Laboratory to set up a demo
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:63
//## guidance-computer unit with which to impress visitors to the lab.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:64
//## Of course, this demo would have to <i>do</i> something, if it was going to be
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:65
//## at all impressive, and to do something it would need some software. In
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:66
//## short order, some of the coders threw together a demo program,
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:67
//## inventing and using the verb/noun user-interface concept (in the
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:68
//## whimsical fashion seen in much of this code), but without any idea
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:69
//## that the verb/noun concept would somehow survive into the flight
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:70
//## software.  As time passed, and more and more people became familiar
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:71
//## with the demo, nobody got around to inventing an improvement for the
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:72
//## user interface, so the coders simply built it into the flight software
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:73
//## without any specific requirements to do so.<br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:74
//## <br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:75
//## However, that does not mean that the verb/noun interface was universally
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:76
//## beloved.  Ram&oacute;n says that <i>many</i> objections were received from
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:77
//## naysayers, such as "it's not scientific", "it's not dignified", or
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:78
//## even "astronauts won't understand it".  Even though the coders of
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:79
//## the demo hadn't seriously intended the verb/noun interface to be used
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:80
//## in any permanent way, it became a kind of devilish game to counter
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:81
//## these objections with (perhaps) sophistic arguments as to why the
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:82
//## interface was really a good one.  In the end, the coders won.  I don't
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:83
//## know whether they were elated or dismayed by this victory.<br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:84
//## <br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:85
//## The astronauts, of course, <i>could</i> understand the interface,
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:86
//## but they did not like it.  Most of them really wanted an interface much
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:87
//## more like that they had used in aircraft:  i.e., lots of dials and
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:88
//## switches.  Dave Scott is the the only astronaut I'm aware of who had
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:89
//## kind words for it (or for the AGC in general), though we are told that
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:90
//## Jim McDivitt wasn't necessary completely hostile to it.<br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:91
//## <br>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:92
//## <div style="text-align: right;"><small>&mdash;Ron Burkey, 07/2009</small></div>
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:93
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:94
//# CALLING SEQUENCES --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:95
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:96
//# KEYBOARD:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:97
//# EACH DEPRESSION OF A KEYBOARD BUTTON ACTIVATES AN INTERRUPT KEYRUPT1
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:98
//# AND PLACES THE 5 BIT KEY CODE INTO CHANNEL 15.  KEYRUPT1 PLACES THE KEY
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:99
//# CODE INTO MPAC, ENTERS AN EXECUTIVE REQUEST FOR THE KEYBOARD AND DISPLAY
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:100
//# PROGRAM (AT 'CHARIN'), AND EXECUTES A RESUME.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:101
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:102
//# UPLINK:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:103
//# EACH WORD RECEIVED BY THE UPLINK ACTIVATES INTERRUPT UPRUPT, WHICH
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:104
//# PLACES THE 5 BIT KEY CODE INTO MPAC, ENTERS AN EXECUTIVE REQUEST FOR THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:105
//# KEYBOARD AND DISPLAY PROGRAM (AT 'CHARIN') AND EXECUTES A RESUME.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:106
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:107
//# INTERNAL PROGRAMS:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:108
//# INTERNAL PROGRAMS CALL PINBALL AT `NVSUB' WITH THE DESIRED VERB/NOUN
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:109
//# CODE IN A (LOW 7 BITS FOR NOUN, NEXT 7 BITS FOR VERB).  DETAILS
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:110
//# DESCRIBED ON REMARKS CARDS JUST BEFORE 'NVSUB' AND 'NVSBWAIT' (SEE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:111
//# SYMBOL TABLE FOR PAGE NUMBERS).
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:112
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:113
//# NORMAL EXIT MODES --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:114
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:115
//#	IF PINBALL WAS CALLED BY EXTERNAL ACTION, THERE ARE FOUR EXITS:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:116
//#		1)	ALL BUT (2), (3), AND (4) EXIT DIRECTLY TO ENDOFJOB.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:117
//#		2)	EXTENDED VERBS GO TO THE EXTENDED VERB FAN AS PART OF THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:118
//# Page 391
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:119
//#			PINBALL EXECUTIVE JOB WITH PRIORITY 30000.  IT IS THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:120
//#			RESPONSIBILITY OF THE EXTEDED VERB CALLED TO EVENTUALLY
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:121
//#			CHANGE PRIORITY (IF NECESSARY) AND DO AN ENDOFJOB.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:122
//#			ALSO PINBALL IS A NOVAC JOB.  EBANK SET FOR COMMON.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:123
//#		3)	VERB 37.  CHANGE OF PROGRAM (MAJOR MODE) CALLS 'V37' IN THE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:124
//#			SERVICE ROUTINES AS PART OF THE PINBALL EXEC JOB WITH PRIO
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:125
//#			30000.  THE NEW PROGRAM CODE (MAJOR MODE) IS LEFT IN A.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:126
//#		4)	KEY RELEASE BUTTON CALLS 'PINBRNCH' IN THE DISPLAY INTERFACE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:127
//#			ROUTINES AS PART OF THE PINBALL EXEC JOB WITH PRIO 30000 IF
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:128
//#			THE KEY RELEASE LIGHT IS OFF AND 'CADRSTOR' IS NOT +0.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:129
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:130
//# IF PINBALL WAS CALLED BY INTERNAL PROGRAMS, EXIT FROM PINBALL IS BACK
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:131
//# TO CALLING ROUTINE.  DETAILS DESCRIBED IN REMARKS CARDS JUST BEFORE
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:132
//# 'NVSUB' AND 'NVSBWAIT' (SEE SYMBOL TABLE FOR PAGE NUMBERS).
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:133
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:134
//# ALARM OR ABORT EXIT MODES --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:135
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:136
//# 	EXTERNAL INITIATION:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:137
//#	IF SOME IMPROPER SEQUENCE OF KEY CODES IS DETECTED, THE OPERATOR
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:138
//#	ERROR LIGHT IS TURNED ON AND EXIT IS TO 'ENDOFJOB'.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:139
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:140
//#	INTERNAL PROGRAM INITIATION:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:141
//#	IF AN ILLEGAL V/N COMBINATION IS ATTEMPTED, AN ABORT IS CAUSED
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:142
//#	(WITH OCTAL 01501).
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:143
//#	IF A SECOND ATTEMPT IS MADE TO GO TO SLEEP IN PINBALL, AN ABORT IS
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:144
//#	CAUSED (WITH OCTAL 01206). THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO GO TO SLEEP IN PINBALL:
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:145
//#		1) ENDIDLE OR DATAWAIT.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:146
//#		2) NVSBWAIT, PRENVBSY, OR NVSUBUSY.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:147
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:148
//# CONDITIONS LEADING TO THE ABOVE ARE DESCRIBED IN FORTHCOMING MIT/IL
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:149
//# E-REPORT DESCRIBING KEYBOARD AND DISPLAY OPERATION FOR 278.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:150
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:151
//# OUTPUT --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:152
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:153
//# INFORMATION TO BE SENT TO THE DISPLAY PANEL IS LEFT IN THE 'DSPTAB'
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:154
//# BUFFERS REGISTERS (UNDER EXEC CONTROL). 'DSPOUT' (A PART OF T4RUPT)
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:155
//# HANDLES THE PLACING OF THE 'DSPTAB' INFORMATION INTO OUTPUT CHANNEL 10
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:156
//# IN INTERRUPT.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:157
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:158
//# ERASABLE INITIALIZATION --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:159
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:160
//# FRESH START AND RESTART INITIALIZE THE NECESSARY E REGISTERS FOR
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:161
//# PINBALL IN 'STARTSUB'.  REGISTERS ARE:  DSPTAB BUFFER, CADRSTOR,
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:162
//# REQRET, CLPASS, DSPLOCK, MONSAVE, MONSAVE1, VERBREG, NOUNREG, DSPLIST,
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:163
//# DSPCOUNT, NOUT.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:164
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:165
//# A COMPLETE LIST OF ALL THE ERASABLES (BOTH RESERVED AND TEMPORARIES) FOR
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:166
//# Page 392
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:167
//# PINBALL IS GIVEN BELOW.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:168
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:169
//# THE FOLLOWING ARE OF GENERAL INTEREST --
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:170
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:171
//#	REMARKS CARDS PRECEDE THE REFERENCED SYMBOL DEFINITION.  SEE SYMBOL
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:172
//# TABLE TO FIND APPROPRIATE PAGE NUMBERS.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:173
//#
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:174
//#	NVSUB		CALLING POINT FOR INTERNAL USE OF PINBALL.
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:175
//#			OF RELATED INTEREST	NVSBWAIT
// PORT: SOURCE PINBALL_GAME_BUTTONS_AND_LIGHTS.agc:176
//#						NVSUBUSY
